.TH RADIO 3
.SH NAME
radio \- packet radio
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind -a '#W' /dev
.PP
.B /dev/radio0/data
.br
.B /dev/radio0/ctl
.br
.B /dev/radio0/stat
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I radio
device serves a directory per radio interface, each holding
.BR data ,
.B ctl
and
.BR stat .
The driver behind it is for the Semtech SX127x family in LoRa mode \- the
part an HopeRF RFM9x module is built around \- reached over SPI.
.PP
.B Data
is message-delimited.  One write is one packet on the air; one read is
one packet received, blocking until there is one.  It is not a byte
stream, because a radio is not one and because what is layered above
depends on the boundary surviving:
.IR ssl (3)
is a filter over a file descriptor, and Styx is a request and a reply.
.PP
Two things are refused rather than accommodated.
.TP
.B "a short read"
A read whose buffer is smaller than the waiting packet fails, naming the
size the packet needs, and the packet stays queued for a read that fits.
It is not truncated.
.IR Ssl (3)
states the contract this inherits \- as long as reads specify buffers of
equal or greater length than the writes at the other end, one write
corresponds to one read \- and a device that quietly returned the first
.I n
bytes would break it somewhere else entirely.
.TP
.B "an oversize write"
A write longer than the payload budget fails and names the budget.  It
is not split across two packets.  Splitting is a segmentation layer, and
a device that did it silently would hide the question of what message
size a link can carry.
.PP
The
.B iounit
of
.B data
is that budget, which is 251 bytes: 255 of FIFO less the four-byte
RadioHead header.  That matters beyond documentation, because
.I exportfs
adopts a transport's iounit when negotiating a Styx message size, so a
server over this settles on a radio-sized message without being told.
.PP
.B Ctl
takes one command per write:
.TP
.BI freq " hz"
Carrier frequency.
.TP
.BI power " dbm"
Transmit power, 2 to 17, through the PA_BOOST output an RFM9x bonds out.
.TP
.BI sf " n"
Spreading factor, 6 to 12.
.TP
.BI bw " hz"
Bandwidth; one of the values the part has, 7800 through 500000.
.TP
.BI cr " n"
Coding rate denominator, 5 to 8.
.TP
.BI node " n"
This station's RadioHead address.
.TP
.BI dest " n"
The default destination address; 255 is broadcast.
.TP
.B reset
Put the part back in sleep and reapply the settings.
.TP
.BI ack " on|off"
Send reliably and acknowledge what arrives.  Off by default, because a
link with one end acknowledging and the other not is worse than neither.
.TP
.BI retries " n"
How many times a reliable send is attempted before it gives up.
.TP
.BI acktimeout " ms"
How long to wait for each acknowledgement.
.PP
.B Ctl 's
.B sf
also sets LowDataRateOptimize, which the datasheet (4.1.1.6) requires
whenever a symbol lasts longer than 16ms \- SF11 and SF12 at 125kHz.
It is mandatory rather than advisory, because the receiver has to agree:
a link with one end setting it and the other not does not work.
.PP
With
.B ack
on, a write is a reliable datagram in the manner of
.IR lib/pycubed_rfm9x.py 's
.B send_with_ack ,
because that is what the other end of a PyCubed link speaks: the
header's identifier byte carries a sequence number, the sender waits for
a packet with the ACK flag and the same identifier, and a
retransmission sets the RETRY flag.  A receiver replies to anything
addressed to it that is not itself an acknowledgement, with the payload
.B !
and the sender's identifier, and it does so from a separate process
because a reply is a transmit and a transmit sleeps.  A broadcast is
neither acknowledged nor waited for.
.PP
A write returns the number of bytes sent, or fails, and with
.B ack
on it fails only after every retry has been tried.  That is the
difference between this and an unacknowledged write, which cannot fail
for any reason the other end would know about.
.PP
A transmit waits for the time the packet will really be on the air,
computed from the spreading factor, bandwidth, coding rate and length,
and not for a constant.  At 125kHz a 20-byte packet is 57ms at SF7 and
1319ms at SF12, so a constant is either wrong at one end or pointlessly
long at the other.
.PP
.B Stat
returns the parameters in force, the RSSI and SNR of the last packet
received, and counters for packets sent and received, CRC errors,
oversize writes, packets heard but addressed elsewhere
.RB ( notours )
packets too short to carry a header
.RB ( runt ),
transmits that never reported done
.RB ( txtimeout )
the computed airtime of a 20-byte packet in the current configuration
.RB ( airtime-20B-ms ),
and, when
.B ack
is on, acknowledgements sent and received
.RB ( acksent ,
.BR ackrecv ),
retransmissions
.RB ( retried )
and sends that gave up
.RB ( sendfail ).
The last two matter more than they look.  A sender that does not speak
the RadioHead header \- a bare LoRa transmitter, which Semtech's own
driver is \- is heard and dropped, and without a counter that is
indistinguishable from a dead link: received stays 0 either way.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/inferno/radio/devradio.c
.br
.B /os/inferno/radio/sx127x.c
.br
.B /os/inferno/mps2/spipl022.c
.br
.B /os/inferno/raspi3b/spipiradio.c
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A kernel with no radio fitted serves an empty
.B #W
directory; the driver says so on the console at boot, naming the
RegVersion it read, because a bus that is not reaching the part reads
.B 0x00
or
.B 0xff
and those are worth telling apart from a different chip.
.SH BUGS
A reliable send holds the part for the whole exchange, so an
acknowledgement this station owes somebody else waits until it finishes.
On a link with one conversation at a time that is invisible; on a busier
one it would cost retransmissions at the other end.
.PP
Duplicate suppression is not implemented.  A retransmission whose
acknowledgement was lost is delivered twice, where
.I lib/pycubed_rfm9x.py
has the beginnings of a seen-identifier table for the same case, also
unfinished.
.PP
Only one interface exists.  A PyCubed has two radio sockets on one SPI
bus, RF1 and RF2; this drives the first.
.PP
Two boards run this, both under a QEMU patched to model an SX1276:
.B mps2
on its Shield0 SPI header and
.B raspi3b
on SPI0
.RB ( tools/qemu-patches ,
0027 to 0036).  Both are fixtures \- an MPS2 has the expansion header
and no radio on it, and a Pi has a header and whatever is plugged into
it \- so what is tested is the register, FIFO and interrupt sequencing
and the file interface above them, and not any real board's bus.
.PP
Of the two,
.B mps2-an386
is the one to test on: it is a Cortex-M4, which is what a SAMD51 is, and
a PyCubed is the target.
.B mps2-an500
is the same board with a Cortex-M7, whose double precision a SAMD51 does
not have, and
.B raspi3b
is a 64-bit Cortex-A53 with a gigabyte of memory, which is nothing like
either.  The Pi is there because it is the board a real part can be
plugged into, not because it resembles the destination.
.PP
The chip and the device are in
.BR /os/inferno/radio ,
beside
.B /os/inferno/ip
and for the same reason: they are shared by more than one machine and
are hardware, so they belong in neither
.B port
nor any one machine's directory.  What is in a machine's directory is
its own half \- the controller, the chip select, which pin the interrupt
arrived on \- and that is the part thrown away when the machine
changes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR radiochk (1),
.IR ssl (3)
